      "SetLogger.jsx: addSelectedExercises/addExercise/replaceExercise had no guard against concurrent re-entrant calls -- each is a loop of real addWorkoutExercise DB inserts with several awaits in between, so a double-fire (a tap replayed by the browser right as the app loses/regains focus, or any other duplicate event) ran the whole insert loop again with the same picks, producing genuine duplicate workout_exercises rows rather than a rendering glitch. Added addingExercisesRef (checked/set synchronously before any await, so a same-tick re-entrant call can't slip through ahead of a state-driven re-render) plus an addingExercises state to disable/relabel the Add button (\"Adding…\") while a previous add is still in flight.",
      "SetLogger.jsx openWizard: weightRef.current.focus() moved out of a setTimeout(..., 60) and into a flushSync(() => { ...state updates... }) block, with focus() called synchronously right after -- same tap handler, no macrotask boundary. The setTimeout was there to wait for the wizard's DOM (including the weight input) to actually mount before focusing it, but that delay pushed the focus() call outside what some mobile browsers (notably iOS Safari) still count as the original trusted user gesture, so the on-screen keyboard sometimes didn't appear even though the input was legitimately focused. flushSync forces the DOM commit synchronously in the same call stack instead, so the focus() call is never separated from the tap by a macrotask.",
